Part 3 – Component Investigation
For this task, you need to investigate six computer components, explain what each one does, and identify an important specification. You should also take or find a photograph of each component and save the images in the images/components/ folder.

Component investigation table
Component

Function

Key Specification

CPU (Central Processing Unit)

The CPU processes instructions and performs calculations. It is often called the brain of the computer because it carries out instructions from software.

Clock speed: Measured in GHz, indicating how many clock cycles the CPU performs per second.

RAM (Random Access Memory)

RAM temporarily stores data and programs that the computer is currently using. More RAM helps a computer run multiple applications smoothly.

Capacity: Measured in GB, such as 8 GB, 16 GB, or 32 GB.

Storage (SSD or HDD)

Storage saves the operating system, applications, documents, photos, and other files, even when the computer is switched off.

Capacity: Measured in GB or TB. SSDs are generally faster than HDDs.

Motherboard

The motherboard is the main circuit board that connects the CPU, RAM, storage, and other components so they can communicate with each other.

Form factor: Such as ATX, Micro-ATX, or Mini-ITX, which determines the board's size and compatibility.

Power Supply (PSU)

The power supply converts electricity from a wall socket into the appropriate voltages needed by the computer's components.

Wattage: Measured in watts (W), such as 550 W or 750 W.

Cooling

Cooling components, such as fans and heatsinks, remove heat from the CPU and other parts to prevent overheating.

Cooling type: Air cooling or liquid cooling.

Photographs and file organisation
Save one photograph for each component in the following folder:

images/components/

