Part 5 – Final Reflection
During this lab, I learned about different computer hardware components and how they work together to make a computer function properly. I discovered that every component has a specific role, including the CPU, RAM, storage, motherboard, power supply, and cooling system. What surprised me most was how all these components communicate with each other to process information and run applications. I also learned that the performance of a computer depends on the specifications and compatibility of its hardware components.

Some components were new to me, especially the motherboard connections, GPIO pins, and the System on Chip (SoC) used in the Raspberry Pi. I learned that GPIO pins allow the Raspberry Pi to connect to external electronic components, such as sensors, LEDs, and motors. I also discovered that the SoC combines important processing functions into a single chip, helping to make the Raspberry Pi compact and energy-efficient.

One challenge I faced during this lab was identifying the different hardware components and understanding their functions. Some components looked similar, so I needed to study their features carefully. Identifying the Raspberry Pi's ports and connections was also challenging at first. However, looking at pictures and labelled diagrams helped me understand the components more clearly.

Overall, this lab improved my knowledge of computer hardware and embedded systems. I learned that embedded computers, such as the Raspberry Pi, are designed to perform specific tasks while using less space and power than many traditional desktop computers. I also gained a better understanding of how hardware and software work together. This knowledge will help me in future computing projects, particularly those involving electronics, programming, and embedded systems.
